
a) Get All Pods in a Namespace
kubectl get pods -n <namespace>

b) Get Pods by Name (or Partial Name)
kubectl get pods | grep <pod-name-part>


a) Delete by Exact Pod Name
kubectl delete pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>
kubectl delete pod my-app-5f9c7d8d9d-abcde -n default

1. Get Logs of a Pod
kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace>
kubectl logs my-app-7d8c9f8b6d-xyz12 -n dev


What kubectl describe Does

It shows detailed information about a Kubernetes resource
 (like Pod, Deployment, Service, Node, etc.).

Unlike kubectl get, which shows summary info, kubectl describe shows events,
conditions, labels, annotations, volumes, status, etc.

1. Describe a Pod
kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>
👉 Useful for checking:

Pod’s container images

Node it’s running on

Conditions (Ready, Running, CrashLoopBackOff)

Events (like scheduling errors, failed pulls)